Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>14  The notepad and links</H1>
<HR>
This chapter adds the Notepad - pages of notes you type straight onto, with a contents page to find them by -
and two things that tie the organiser together. The Link tool joins any two entries (a lunch in the Diary and
the person you're having it with, say), so that one leads to the other; and a Back button takes you back to
where you were before you followed a link or turned to another section. The result is <CODE>!Org14</CODE>.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O14Notes" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Pages of notes</H2>
A page of the notepad is a title, its text, and its place in the notepad:<P>
<PRE>
{ id: 2, title: 'Holiday ideas', order: 1,
  text: 'Cornwall: St Ives in June?\nThe Lake District ...' }
</PRE>
The text is a string with <CODE>\n</CODE> between its lines. The pages are kept in a collection like every
other section's, with two additions:<P>
<PRE>
// A page of the notepad: { title, text, order }
export class Notes extends Collection {
  list() { return this.all().sort((a, b) =&gt; a.order - b.order); }
  /** Move a page to position i (0 = first). */
  moveTo(id, i) {
    const pages = this.list().filter((p) =&gt; p.id !== id);
    pages.splice(i, 0, this.get(id));
    pages.forEach((p, k) =&gt; {
      if (p.order !== k) this.update(p.id, { order: k });
    });
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>list</CODE> gives the pages in order. <CODE>moveTo</CODE> moves a page to another place: it takes the
page out of the list, puts it back at position <CODE>i</CODE>, and then renumbers every page whose
<CODE>order</CODE> has changed.<P>
<H3>splice</H3>
Putting the page back uses an array method you haven't needed before. <CODE>splice(i, n, ...items)</CODE>
changes an array in place: at position <CODE>i</CODE> it takes out <CODE>n</CODE> items and puts in whatever
follows. With <CODE>n</CODE> 0 it only puts things in:<P>
<PRE>
const a = ['A', 'B'];
a.splice(0, 0, 'C');       // a is now ['C', 'A', 'B']
a.splice(1, 1);            // takes out 'A': ['C', 'B']
</PRE>
Only the pages whose number really changes are updated, so moving a page near the end of a long notepad
doesn't mark every page as changed. Check tests it:<P>
<PRE>
  const notes = new Notes([{ id: 1, title: 'A', order: 0 },
    { id: 2, title: 'B', order: 1 },
    { id: 3, title: 'C', order: 2 }]);
  notes.moveTo(3, 0);
  check('notepad: moving a page', notes.list().map((p) =&gt; p.title),
    ['C', 'A', 'B']);
</PRE>
and the Document makes a <CODE>Notes</CODE> collection from the file's <CODE>notes</CODE> (and, for the links
later in this chapter, a <CODE>Links</CODE> collection from its <CODE>links</CODE>):<P>
<PRE>
    this.notes = new Notes(data.notes);
    this.planner = new Collection(data.planner);    // (kept)
    this.anniversaries = new Anniversaries(data.anniversaries);
    this.links = new Links(data.links);
</PRE>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The contents page</H2>
The left-hand page lists the pages' titles, numbered, with the one you're reading highlighted; the right-hand
page shows that page:<P>
<PRE>
export class NotesPage extends Section {
  constructor(org) {
    super(org, { id: 'notes', name: 'Notepad', colour: '#f6b26b',
      newHelp: 'add a page to the notepad' });
    this.page = null;                 // the id of the page shown
    this.area = null;                 // its TextArea, while shown
  }
  state() { return { page: this.page }; }
  restore(s) { this.hide(); Object.assign(this, s); this.show(); }

  draw(g, L, R) {
    heading(g, L, 'Notepad', this.colour, 'Contents');
    ruled(g, L, L.y + TOP + ROW, ROW);
    const pages = this.doc.notes.list();
    pages.forEach((p, i) =&gt; {
      const y = L.y + TOP + i * ROW;
      if (p.id === this.page) {
        g.fillStyle = '#fdebd3';
        g.fillRect(L.x + 10, y, L.w - 20, ROW);
      }
      text(g, `${i + 1}  ${p.title}`, L.x + 14, y + ROW / 2, L.w - 28,
        '13px Homerton');
      this.box(L.x + 10, y, L.w - 20, ROW, { id: p.id });
    });
    if (!pages.length) {
      text(g, 'No pages yet. Click the New button to add one.',
        L.x + 14, L.y + TOP + 12, L.w - 28, 'italic 12px Homerton',
        FAINT);
    }
    const p = this.doc.notes.get(this.page);
    heading(g, R, p ? p.title : 'Notepad', this.colour);
    if (!p) {
      text(g, 'Click a title on the contents page to turn to it.',
        R.x + 14, R.y + TOP + 12, R.w - 28, 'italic 12px Homerton',
        FAINT);
    }
  }
</PRE>
Each title is recorded with <CODE>this.box</CODE>, as the To do page does with its rows, so a click can find
it - and so choosing, linking and the wastebasket work on notepad pages too. <CODE>this.page</CODE> is the id
of the page shown on the right, or <CODE>null</CODE> for none.<P>
The right-hand page draws only its heading. The text itself isn't drawn by <CODE>draw</CODE> at all: it is in a
<CODE>TextArea</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Typing on the page</H2>
Writing a multi-line text editor is a job in itself (chapter 17 shows how to make a small one), so the notepad
uses the one the desktop provides: the <CODE>TextArea</CODE> you met in !Contacts' card in chapter 3, where it
held the address and the notes (appendix C has the details). As there, it draws itself on a canvas of its own
in the window's work area, wraps words, scrolls, and takes clicks inside it and keys while it has the caret.
This time it isn't in a Form, so the Notepad makes it itself: it gives it a position and a size, and listens
for its <CODE>'change'</CODE> event:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the text area
  /** Where the page's text goes on the right-hand page. */
  get rect() {
    const R = this.org.pages[1];
    return { x: R.x + 12, y: R.y + TOP - 4, w: R.w - 24,
      h: R.h - TOP - 36 };
  }
  show() {
    const p = this.doc.notes.get(this.page);
    if (!p || this.area) return;
    this.area = new TextArea(this.org.win, { ...this.rect,
      text: p.text, font: '14px Homerton' });
    this.area.on('change', ({ text }) =&gt; {
      this.doc.notes.update(p.id, { text });
    });
  }
  hide() { this.area?.remove(); this.area = null; }
  open(id) {
    this.hide();
    this.page = id;
    this.show();
    this.area?.focus();
    this.org.redraw();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>rect</CODE> is the space on the right-hand page below the heading - a getter, because the page's
position comes from the binder (<CODE>this.org.pages</CODE>). <CODE>show</CODE> makes the TextArea with the
page's text, and every change puts the new text straight into the collection, which marks the document as
changed (the <CODE>*</CODE> in the title) as any other change does. <CODE>hide</CODE> takes the TextArea away.
<CODE>open</CODE> turns to a page: it hides the old one, shows the new one and gives it the caret, so you can
type at once.<P>
The window calls a section's <CODE>show</CODE> and <CODE>hide</CODE> (chapter 10) when you open it and when
you go to another tab, so the TextArea is only there while the Notepad is.<P>

<H3>Who gets the keys first</H3>
The notepad page lives in the organiser's window, and that window already has click and key handlers of its
own: a click chooses an entry, and keys do things - Delete deletes the chosen entry, Page Up turns the page,
Insert adds an entry:<P>
<PRE>
  key(ev) {
    if (this.section.key(ev)) return true;
    switch (ev.code) {
      case 0x183: this.doc.quickSave(); return true;         // F3
      case 0x19F: this.section.turn(-1); break;          // Page Up
      case 0x19E: this.section.turn(1); break;           // Page Down
      case 30: this.section.today(); break;                  // Home
      case 0x1CD: this.section.newEntry(); return true;      // Insert
      case 127: case 8: tools.remove(this); return true;     // Delete
</PRE>
Now imagine typing a note and pressing Backspace. The key goes to the window, and if the window's handler saw
it first, it would delete the chosen <I>entry</I> instead of a letter. The TextArea must see the key first,
and only let it go on to the window if it doesn't want it.<P>
It does, because of how it adds its handlers. In chapter 1 the <CODE>Emitter</CODE> called handlers in the
order they were added. The desktop's windows use the same idea, with one extra: a handler added with
<CODE>{ first: true }</CODE> goes in front of the others. The TextArea adds its handlers like that:<P>
<PRE>
win.on('click', (ev) =&gt; this.click(ev), { first: true });
win.on('key', (ev) =&gt; this.key(ev), { first: true });
</PRE>
Handlers stop as soon as one returns <CODE>true</CODE>. The TextArea returns <CODE>true</CODE> for a click
inside itself and for a key it uses while it has the caret, so the window never sees those; everything else -
a click on a title, a key like F3 while the note has the caret - goes on to the window as usual. That's why
the Notepad's own <CODE>click</CODE> and <CODE>key</CODE> are so short:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ mouse and keys
  // (the TextArea sees clicks in it and keys while it has the caret
  // before the window does, so only the rest come here)
  click(ev) {
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (box) { this.open(box.ref.id); return true; }
    return false;
  }
  key(ev) {
    if (!this.area?.focused || ev.code !== 4) return false;  // Ctrl-D
    this.area.insert(D.format(D.today(), '%ZDY %MO %CE%YR'));
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
The opposite matters as much. When the page is put away, the TextArea's handlers must go too, or a
TextArea no longer on the screen would go on taking the window's clicks and keys. So <CODE>remove</CODE> -
called by the Notepad's <CODE>hide</CODE> - takes its handlers away before its canvas. The desktop's
<CODE>on</CODE> returns a function that removes the handler again (like the Emitter's <CODE>off</CODE>), and
the TextArea keeps those functions and calls them all (shortened a little here):<P>
<PRE>
remove() {
  for (const off of this._offs) off();
  this.canvas.remove();
}
</PRE>
Anything that adds handlers to someone else's window should be as tidy.<P>

<H3>Ctrl-D: today's date</H3>
The Style Guide gives Ctrl-D the job of typing the date. The TextArea doesn't know that key, so it goes on to
the window, which asks the section first (<CODE>this.section.key(ev)</CODE> above). The Notepad's
<CODE>key</CODE> checks that its TextArea has the caret and the key is code 4 - Ctrl with a letter gives the
letter's place in the alphabet, and D is the fourth - and puts the date in at the caret with
<CODE>insert</CODE>. <CODE>D.format</CODE> is chapter 11's, which uses the desktop's <CODE>formatTime</CODE>
codes: <CODE>%ZDY %MO %CE%YR</CODE> is the day without a leading zero, the month's name and the year, as in
<I>25 September 2026</I>.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Adding, renaming and moving pages</H2>
New (the toolbox button, or Insert) asks for a title and opens the new page, and double-clicking a title
changes it:<P>
<PRE>
  async double(ev) {
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (!box) return;
    const p = this.doc.notes.get(box.ref.id);
    const title = await askTitle(this.org.app.task, p.title);
    this.org.focus();
    if (title) this.doc.notes.update(p.id, { title });
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------ entries
  async newEntry() {
    const title = await askTitle(this.org.app.task);
    this.org.focus();
    if (!title) return;
    const p = this.doc.notes.add({ title, text: '',
      order: this.doc.notes.size });
    this.open(p.id);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>askTitle</CODE> is a one-field form in <CODE>Entries</CODE>, beside the others:<P>
<PRE>
export async function askTitle(task, title = '') {
  const f = form(task, 'title', {
    title: 'Notepad page', buttons: ['Cancel', 'OK'],
    fields: [{ name: 'title', label: 'Title' }],
    check: (v) =&gt; (!v.title.trim() ? { field: 'title',
      message: 'Please give the page a title' } : null),
  });
  const v = await f.ask({ title });
  return v &amp;&amp; v.title.trim();
}
</PRE>
Pages are moved by dragging their titles. Chapter 12's <CODE>Tools.drag</CODE> handles every drag of an entry:
into the wastebasket it deletes, and anywhere else on the pages it calls the section's
<CODE>dropped</CODE>. The Notepad works out which row the title was dropped on and moves the page there:<P>
<PRE>
  /** A title dropped further up or down the contents: move it. */
  dropped(ref, x, y) {
    const L = this.org.pages[0];
    const i = Math.max(0, Math.floor((y - L.y - TOP) / ROW));
    this.doc.notes.moveTo(ref.id, Math.min(i,
      this.doc.notes.size - 1));
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>Math.max</CODE> and <CODE>Math.min</CODE> keep the row between the first page and the last, wherever the
title is dropped.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Linking entries</H2>
Some entries belong together: a lunch in the Diary and the person it's with, a to-do and the notepad page with
its details. A <I>link</I> joins two of them. It is kept as the two entries' refs - the
<CODE>{ section, id }</CODE> objects chapter 10 introduced (the ones <CODE>box</CODE> records), which chapter 12
used to reach another section's entries - in a collection of its own:<P>
<PRE>
// A link between two entries: { a: ref, b: ref }, where a ref is
// { section: 'diary', id }
export const sameRef = (p, q) =&gt;
  p.section === q.section &amp;&amp; p.id === q.id;
export class Links extends Collection {
  /** The entries linked to ref. */
  of(ref) {
    return this.all().flatMap((l) =&gt; (sameRef(l.a, ref) ? [l.b]
      : sameRef(l.b, ref) ? [l.a] : []));
  }
  has(ref) { return this.of(ref).length &gt; 0; }
  link(a, b) {
    if (sameRef(a, b)) return;
    if (this.of(a).some((r) =&gt; sameRef(r, b))) return;
    this.add({ a, b });
  }
  /** Forget the links of an entry that has gone. */
  forget(ref) {
    for (const l of this.all()) {
      if (sameRef(l.a, ref) || sameRef(l.b, ref)) this.remove(l.id);
    }
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>sameRef</CODE> says whether two refs are the same entry. A link has no direction, so <CODE>of</CODE> looks
at both ends: for each link, if one end is the entry, the other end is linked to it. <CODE>flatMap</CODE>
(chapter 9) makes that easy - each link gives an array of one ref, or of none, and <CODE>flatMap</CODE> joins
them into one array.<P>
<CODE>link</CODE> won't join an entry to itself, or join two entries twice (either way round);
<CODE>forget</CODE> removes the links of an entry that's been deleted, so no link is left pointing at nothing.
Deleting calls it, in <CODE>Tools.remove</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
  s.remove(ref);
  org.doc.links.forget(ref);
  org.selected = null;
  org.refresh();
</PRE>
Check makes sure of the rules:<P>
<PRE>
  const links = new Links();
  const a = { section: 'diary', id: 1 };
  const b = { section: 'todo', id: 2 };
  links.link(a, b);
  links.link(b, a);                       // (the same link again)
  check('links both ways, once',
    [links.of(a), links.of(b), links.size], [[b], [a], 1]);
  links.forget(b);
  check('links forgotten', links.has(a), false);
</PRE>

<H3>The Link tool</H3>
The toolbox gets a Link button (and the window's menu a <I>Link</I> item). Choose an entry and click Link, or
click Link and then an entry; then click the entry to link it to:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ links
export function startLink(org) {
  org.linking = org.selected ? { first: org.selected } : {};
  org.refresh();
}
function pickForLink(org, ref) {
  if (!org.linking.first) org.linking.first = ref;
  else {
    org.doc.links.link(org.linking.first, ref);
    org.linking = null;
  }
  org.refresh();
}
</PRE>
<CODE>org.linking</CODE> is <CODE>null</CODE> normally. While the tool is in use it is an object holding the
first entry once it's been chosen. Every click on an entry now goes through <CODE>clickEntry</CODE>, which
hands it to the Link tool while it's on:<P>
<PRE>
/** A click on an entry: the Link tool, a chain, or choosing it. */
export function clickEntry(org, ev) {
  const box = org.section.at(ev.x, ev.y);
  if (!box) {
    if (org.linking) { org.linking = null; org.refresh(); }
    org.selected = null;
    return false;
  }
  if (org.linking) { pickForLink(org, box.ref); return true; }
  if (org.doc.links.has(box.ref) &amp;&amp; ev.x &gt; box.x + box.w - 16) {
    linkMenu(org, box.ref, ev);           // the chain mark
    return true;
  }
  org.selected = box.ref;
  org.redraw();
  return false;                           // the section sees it too
}
</PRE>
Clicking somewhere that isn't an entry puts the tool away. The Link button is drawn pressed in while it's on
(<CODE>setState({ selected: !!org.linking })</CODE> when the toolbox is updated), and the first entry is
outlined in red.<P>

<H3>Chains</H3>
An entry with links shows a small chain at its right-hand end. The window draws the chains after the section
has drawn its page, for every entry the section recorded with <CODE>box</CODE> - so every section gets them
without any code of its own:<P>
<PRE>
/** After the section has drawn: chain marks and the chosen entry. */
export function decorate(org, g) {
  const same = (a, b) =&gt; a &amp;&amp; b &amp;&amp; a.section === b.section &amp;&amp;
    a.id === b.id &amp;&amp; (a.date ?? '') === (b.date ?? '');
  for (const b of org.section.boxes) {
    if (org.doc.links.has(b.ref)) chain(g, b.x + b.w - 12, b.y + 8);
    const chosen = same(b.ref, org.selected) ||
      same(b.ref, org.linking?.first);
    if (chosen) {
      g.strokeStyle = org.linking ? '#c00000' : '#000000';
      g.lineWidth = 2;
      g.strokeRect(b.x - 1, b.y - 1, b.w + 2, b.h + 2);
    }
  }
}
</PRE>
The chain is two ellipses, drawn in <CODE>Paper</CODE> with the canvas's <CODE>ellipse(x, y, rx, ry,
rotation, start, end)</CODE>, which is <CODE>arc</CODE> with different widths across and down:<P>
<PRE>
/** A little chain: the mark of an entry that is linked to others. */
export function chain(g, x, y) {
  g.strokeStyle = '#444444';
  g.lineWidth = 1.5;
  for (const dx of [0, 5]) {
    g.beginPath();
    g.ellipse(x + dx, y, 4, 2.5, -0.5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    g.stroke();
  }
}
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O14Link" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
Clicking a chain opens a menu of the entries at the other ends of its links. Each item's text comes from the
section the entry belongs to - its <CODE>describe</CODE>, from chapter 12 - and choosing one goes to it:<P>
<PRE>
function linkMenu(org, ref, ev) {
  const items = org.doc.links.of(ref).map((r) =&gt; {
    const s = org.sections.find((x) =&gt; x.id === r.section);
    return { text: `${s.name}: ${s.describe(r)}`.slice(0, 40),
      action: () =&gt; org.go(r) };
  });
  wimp.menus.openAt(new Menu('Linked to', items), ev,
    { task: org.app.task });
}
</PRE>
<CODE>org.go</CODE> opens the right section and asks it to show the entry (its <CODE>go</CODE>): the Diary
turns to the appointment's week, the Address section to the contact, the Notepad to the page.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Going back</H2>
Following a link can take you anywhere in the organiser, so the toolbox gets a Back button. The window keeps a
<I>history</I>: each time you go somewhere else, it remembers where you were.<P>
<PRE>
  /** Open section i (remembering where we were, for Back). */
  open(i, remember = true) {
    if (i === this.current) return;
    if (remember) this.remember();
    this.section.hide();
    this.current = i;
    this.selected = null;
    this.section.show();
    this.refresh();
  }
  remember() {
    this.history.push({ i: this.current, s: this.section.state() });
    if (this.history.length &gt; 30) this.history.shift();
  }
  back() {
    const h = this.history.pop();
    if (!h) { wimp.beep(); return; }
    this.open(h.i, false);
    this.section.restore(h.s);
    this.refresh();
  }
  /** Show an entry, in whichever section it is. */
  go(ref) {
    const i = this.sections.findIndex((s) =&gt; s.id === ref.section);
    this.remember();
    this.open(i, false);
    this.section.go(ref);
    this.selected = ref;
    this.refresh();
  }
</PRE>
Where you are is two things: which section is open (<CODE>this.current</CODE>) and where that section is - the
Diary's view and day, the Notepad's page. Each section says where it is with <CODE>state</CODE> and goes
back there with <CODE>restore</CODE>. The <CODE>Section</CODE> class gives every section a plain version,
which just remembers the view:<P>
<PRE>
  /** Where the section is (its view ...), for the Back button. */
  state() { return { view: this.view }; }
  restore(s) { Object.assign(this, s); }
</PRE>
and sections with more to remember give their own. The Diary adds its day:<P>
<PRE>
  state() { return { view: this.view, day: this.day }; }
</PRE>
The Notepad remembers its page, and its <CODE>restore</CODE> hides the TextArea before changing page and shows
it again after, so the text on the screen is the page's:<P>
<PRE>
  state() { return { page: this.page }; }
  restore(s) { this.hide(); Object.assign(this, s); this.show(); }
</PRE>
The Address section remembers its letter and the chosen person, and the Anniversary section its year, each
with a one-line <CODE>state</CODE> of the same kind (<CODE>restore</CODE> is the <CODE>Section</CODE> class's,
which copies the fields back):<P>
<PRE>
  state() { return { letter: this.letter, chosen: this.chosen }; }
</PRE>
<PRE>
  state() { return { year: this.year }; }
</PRE>
<CODE>remember</CODE> pushes <CODE>{ i, s }</CODE> onto the history - <CODE>shift</CODE> takes the oldest off
the front once there are 30, so it can't grow for ever - and <CODE>back</CODE> pops the latest one off,
opens that section and restores its state. With nothing to go back to, it beeps.<P>
<H3>Opening without remembering</H3>
<CODE>open(i, remember = true)</CODE> gives its second parameter a default value, as chapter 3's
<CODE>editCard</CODE> did: called as <CODE>open(2)</CODE>, <CODE>remember</CODE> is <CODE>true</CODE>.
<CODE>back</CODE> calls <CODE>open(h.i, false)</CODE>, because going
back mustn't itself be remembered, or Back would go to and fro between the same two places.<P>
<CODE>go</CODE> also calls <CODE>open(i, false)</CODE>, after calling <CODE>remember</CODE> itself. That
isn't the same: <CODE>open</CODE> does nothing at all if the section is already open, so a link from one
diary entry to another wouldn't have been remembered. Calling <CODE>remember</CODE> first means every
<CODE>go</CODE> can be undone with Back. The Diary does the same when a click in the month or year view
turns to a week (<CODE>this.org.remember()</CODE> in its <CODE>click</CODE>).<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Trying it</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Run <CODE>!Org14</CODE> and open the sample <CODE>Diary</CODE>. In the Diary, <I>Lunch with
Bob</I> on Saturday 26 September has a chain: it's linked to Bob's card.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Click the chain and choose <I>Address: Jones, Bob</I> from the menu. The Address section opens at
the letter J with Bob chosen. Click the Back button: you're back at the week.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Click the <I>Notepad</I> tab and click <I>Holiday ideas</I>. Type a line, and press Ctrl-D to add
today's date. Press Backspace: it deletes a letter, not the page.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Drag <I>Gift ideas</I> to the top of the contents: it becomes page 1.<P>
<DT><DD>5  Click <I>Gift ideas</I>, click the Link button, then click the <I>To do</I> tab and click <I>Buy a
birthday card for Bob</I>. Both now have chains.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>The desktop's <CODE>TextArea</CODE> gives a window several lines of editable text; show it while its page
is shown and remove it when it isn't.<P>
<LI>A handler added with <CODE>{ first: true }</CODE> runs before the others, and returning
<CODE>true</CODE> stops the rest: that's how a TextArea takes its keys before the window does.<P>
<LI>Whatever adds handlers to a window must take them away again when it's finished.<P>
<LI>Ctrl with a letter gives the letter's place in the alphabet: Ctrl-D is 4.<P>
<LI><CODE>splice(i, n, ...items)</CODE> takes items out of an array and puts others in, in place.<P>
<LI>A link is two refs; <CODE>sameRef</CODE> compares them, and a deleted entry's links are forgotten.<P>
<LI>A history of <CODE>{ i, s }</CODE> - which section, and where it was - makes a Back button; each section
says where it is with <CODE>state</CODE> and goes back with <CODE>restore</CODE>.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Make Ctrl-T type the time (hours and minutes) in the Notepad, as the Style Guide asks.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Show each page's first line under its title on the contents page, in the faint colour, when the
title is short enough to leave room.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add a <I>Forward</I> button that undoes Back.<P>
<DT><DD>4  A link has no direction. Add a way to remove one: an extra item at the foot of the chain's menu,
<I>Unlink from ...</I> for each linked entry.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  In the Notepad's <CODE>key</CODE>:
<PRE>
if (!this.area?.focused) return false;
if (ev.code === 4) {
  this.area.insert(D.format(D.today(), '%ZDY %MO %CE%YR'));
} else if (ev.code === 20) {                         // Ctrl-T
  this.area.insert(formatTime('%24:%MI', new Date()));
} else return false;
return true;
</PRE>
(importing <CODE>formatTime</CODE> from <CODE>'riscos'</CODE>).<P>
<DT><DD>2  In <CODE>draw</CODE>, for each page, after its title: measure the title in its font, and if
there are at least 60 pixels left, put the first line after it:
<PRE>
g.font = '13px Homerton';
const w = g.measureText(`${i + 1}  ${p.title}`).width;
const room = L.w - 28 - w - 16;
if (room &gt;= 60) {
  const first = (p.text ?? '').split('\n')[0];
  text(g, first, L.x + 14 + w + 16, y + ROW / 2, room,
    'italic 11px Homerton', FAINT);
}
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  Keep a second list, <CODE>this.ahead</CODE>. <CODE>back</CODE> pushes where you are onto it before
going back; a new <CODE>forward</CODE> pops from it, pushes onto <CODE>history</CODE> and opens that place
(the same three lines as <CODE>back</CODE>); and <CODE>remember</CODE> empties <CODE>ahead</CODE>, since
going somewhere new starts a new path.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Give <CODE>Links</CODE> an <CODE>unlink(a, b)</CODE> that removes the link whose ends are
<CODE>a</CODE> and <CODE>b</CODE> either way round, and in <CODE>linkMenu</CODE> add, after the entries:
<PRE>
...org.doc.links.of(ref).map((r) =&gt; ({
  text: `Unlink from ${describe(r)}`.slice(0, 40),
  action: () =&gt; org.doc.links.unlink(ref, r) }))
</PRE>
where <CODE>describe(r)</CODE> is the text the entries' items use. To separate the two groups, give the
<I>last</I> of the entries' items <CODE>dotted: true</CODE>: a menu item's dotted line is drawn below it.<P>
</DL>

